From 81a7298ca1600457b3c9d3545d2a9c0acb078803 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:43:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08866/11362] P6 result: MoE-many does not beat dense-few at
 matched wall-clock

REQ-ARC-WMTE-6254, the last of the P1-P6 directive. MoE wins 0 of 2 comparable
games; pooled dense 0.7595 vs MoE best-of-N 0.1167.

The speed premise held and did not matter. Qwen3.6-35B-A3B's FIRST sample really
was ~2.4x faster than gemma-4-31B (109.7s / 97.8s vs 240.4s / 283.9s), so "more
samples per second" was real. The quality gap swamped it: 0.0797 vs 0.519 on
cn04, 0.1538 vs 1.0 on ls20. Two cheap bad samples do not beat one good one.
This CONFIRMS the 2026-07-28 generator pin under the one frame that decision
never measured, rather than challenging it. It authorizes no pin change.

Caveats kept prominent because they are load-bearing: think mode is default-ON
and Qwen3.6 may emit far more reasoning tokens than gemma, so some wall-clock may
be think-verbosity rather than architecture (unmeasured); n=2 comparable games,
far under the n>=30 bar; and the MoE arm conflates MoE-per-second with best-of-N
selection, which exp6251 just showed is unreliable. Every MoE cell overspent its
budget, which is inherent rather than a defect -- a sample's cost is unknowable
before drawing it -- and is flagged per game. The second-draw slowdown and
s5i5's 836.6s no-candidate outlier are unexplained and were not investigated.

Also fixes a real bug in this script, caught mid-run: it read
scratch/dense_<game>/world_model.py while the proposer writes
E3_DIR/<game>/world_model.py, so every arm returned no candidate across three
dense games and one MoE game (~16 min of GPU, all held=None). A _store_path()
helper now points at the path the proposer actually writes, with the incident in
its docstring. Caught by reading per-game rows rather than the verdict string --
the run would otherwise have reported a plausible zero-comparable-games null.

